    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2023475741514412312",
      "url": "https://x.com/CwNewbie11/status/2023475741514412312",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/CwNewbie11/status/2023475741514412312",
      "text": "Antique find of the day!!!!! In 1898, Hobson took temporary command of a coal ship called the Merrimac. His mission was bold and risky: sink the ship in the narrow entrance to Santiago Harbor in Cuba to block the Spanish fleet inside.\nBefore dawn on June 3, he and his small crew sailed into the harbor under heavy enemy fire. Spanish guns quickly damaged the ship’s steering, making it hard to control. Although Hobson managed to sink the Merrimac, it did not land in the best position to completely block the channel.\nHobson and his six crew members were captured but treated respectfully by Spanish Admiral Cervera.\nWhile he was a prisoner of war in Cuba, American newspapers turned Hobson into a national hero. His picture appeared in papers across the country, often alongside dramatic stories about his bravery. Many people believed he had volunteered for what seemed like a suicide mission. Supporters even raised money to help his parents save their home from foreclosure.\nWhen Hobson was released in a prisoner exchange on July 6, 1898, American soldiers cheered loudly as he passed by. Back home, he received countless invitations to give speeches. After having dinner with President William McKinley, he traveled west by train toward San Francisco and eventually the Philippines. At nearly every stop, crowds gathered to see him.\nHobson became famous not just for his courage, but also for his charm. He often greeted admirers—especially young women—with enthusiastic kisses. Newspapers loved it. During a time when sensational journalism was growing in popularity, Hobson became a full-fledged celebrity and was even nicknamed “the most kissed man in America.”\nThis stereo view card has a rare picture of this man!",
